AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly applied across mental health care, including risk detection, digital phenotyping, treatment personalisation, conversational interventions and population-level surveillance. This critical review synthesises evidence across the mental-health pathway, from early detection and precision intervention to prevention, service planning and equitable real-world implementation. AI models have shown potential to identify patterns associated with depression, psychosis, suicide risk, relapse and treatment response using electronic health records, language, smartphones, wearables and multimodal data. Conversational agents and AI-supported decision systems may expand access, support clinical decision-making and improve service efficiency, while population-level analytics may strengthen surveillance, demand forecasting and resource allocation. However, evidence remains considerably stronger for prediction and model development than for improved clinical outcomes. External validation, prospective multicentre evaluation, comparison with usual care and health-economic analysis remain limited. Poor calibration, unrepresentative datasets, algorithmic and linguistic bias, privacy risks, digital exclusion, automation bias and generative-AI hallucinations may further compromise safe and equitable implementation. Evidence from culturally diverse, underserved and resource-constrained populations is particularly limited. The central challenge is therefore not whether AI can generate accurate predictions, but whether its use produces meaningful, affordable and equitable improvements in care. Translation into practice requires clinical validity, demonstrated clinical utility, safety and equity, effective human integration and lifecycle governance. AI should augment rather than replace professional judgement, patient choice and therapeutic relationships. Its value should ultimately be assessed through real-world outcomes, including safer care, improved health, expanded access and reduced mental-health inequalities.
