AB  - This configurative mapping review synthesizes empirical research on parasocial interactions (PSI) and parasocial relationships (PSR) in science podcasts and examines how these processes relate to digital trust, engagement, and cognitive elaboration. Following a SPIDER-guided search and PRISMA-based screening, we identified and analyzed 13 empirical studies, mapping key methodological choices, conceptual definitions, trust targets, and outcome measures. Across studies, three recurring configurations describe how PSR-relevant dynamics are embedded in science podcasts: (1) credibility- and expertise-related cues that support perceptions of competent sources, (2) narrative and dialogic communication that fosters emotional proximity, and (3) host self-disclosure and voice continuity that strengthen relational stability. Overall, the reviewed evidence suggests that PSI/PSR function as psychosocial mechanisms linking source credibility with affective involvement and information processing in mediated science communication. At the same time, most studies are correlational, which limits causal inference. The findings highlight science podcasts as a distinctive form of computer-mediated communication that combines relational closeness with cognitive learning potential and point to gaps for future research, including system-level trust, more diverse cultural and linguistic contexts, and emerging formats such as AI-mediated hosting.
