AB  - This study examines the conceptual structure of research on artificial intelligence (AI) in smart tourism within business and management research. The review is exploratory and rests on a single database. It draws on 42 journal articles published between 2021 and 2026, retrieved from Scopus in the Business, Management and Accounting subject area. The analysis used the bibliometrix R package, combining performance analysis with keyword co-occurrence and thematic mapping. Output concentrated in 2025 and 2026, with no corpus article in 2024. Citations were highly skewed: the median article held 0.5 citations, the five most cited held 85.4% of the total, and half the corpus remained uncited. Authorship was equally dispersed: 138 of 139 authors contributed a single article. Smart tourism and AI formed the motor theme, while robotics, the metaverse, and virtual reality served as enabling technologies. Sustainable tourism recorded the highest internal development of any cluster (Callon density 89.29), yet it sat in the niche quadrant, weakly connected to the core. Efficiency, generative AI, and human-AI collaboration shared zero centrality and median density, at the periphery of the map. Efficiency was marginal rather than dominant in this corpus, a difference from adjacent robotics reviews that reflects disciplinary boundaries, not contradiction. Quadrant assignment is sensitive to one tie-break rule, because five of the ten clusters sit exactly on the median density, and both readings are reported. The study synthesizes these patterns into an integrative four-layer framework and charts a research agenda for the responsible use of AI in smart tourism.
